# Shared signal list for no-meta-commentary.sh (pre) and
# no-meta-commentary-check.sh (post). One ERE per line, matched case-insensitively
# with `grep -E -i -f`. Comment lines start with # and are stripped before use.
#
# The list is deliberately SHORT and high-precision. It is not a definition of the
# rule and cannot be: the rule is "chat is for chat, not files", and no pattern
# list decides that. These are the shapes measured to be near-absent from real
# authored files, so a hit is worth one line of the agent's attention.
#
# Measured before shipping against a real project-deliverables vault (0 hits)
# and ~/.agents (11 hits, all inside quoting sites the hook path-excludes).
# Phrases that measured noisy in real prose are deliberately absent: bare "this
# document/section/file", bare "supersedes", and any standalone second person.
#
# The document-as-subject block below revisits that first exclusion. Bare "this
# section" is noisy; "this section IS" is not, because the copula is what turns a
# pointer into a claim about the artifact. Measured 2026-08-25 across this repo,
# the installed skills tree and four private markdown corpora: 47 hits, every one
# a genuine document-as-subject construction, no false positives.
# "table" is deliberately NOT in the noun set: it measured 3 hits in Kimball
# fact-table prose, the only noun in the set with a competing technical sense that
# fires in practice.
#
# Word boundaries are load-bearing: without them "note that I" matches inside
# "note that it is", which is ordinary prose. Verified against real files.
#
# First-person narration about the work
\bI've\b
\bI have (created|added|updated|written|removed|left|included|kept|made)\b
\bhere's what I\b
\bnote that I\b
\b(now|next,?) I'll\b
\blet's now\b
# Addressed to the reader rather than describing the subject
\bwould you like me to\b
\blet me know\b
\bfeel free to\b
\bhope this helps\b
\bas requested\b
\bper your request\b
\bas you can see\b
# Headings that report on a session instead of naming a subject
^#+[[:space:]]+.*(summary of changes|what i did|what i changed|changes made|why this matters)
# Apologizing for the artifact inside the artifact, or dating it
\bthis is a subset\b
\bcoverage is partial\b
\bnot yet complete\b
\bthis replaces the earlier\b
^supersedes:
# The document as the subject of its own sentence. A pointer keeps a transitive verb
# ("section 7 carries the chain"); a claim about the artifact takes a copula. The
# sentence-start anchor is what keeps "the jobs in section 7 are..." out, where the
# document noun sits in a prepositional phrase and something else is the subject.
(^|[.!?][[:space:]]+|^[[:space:]]*[*_]+)this[[:space:]]+(section|subsection|document|doc|guide|runbook|register|inventory|spec|specification|readme|chapter|appendix|page|file|note|list|report|matrix)[[:space:]]+(is|are|was|were|stays|remains|becomes|exists)[[:space:]]
(^|[.!?][[:space:]]+)(section|subsection|chapter|appendix)[[:space:]]+[0-9]+[[:space:]]+(is|are|was|were|stays|remains|becomes|exists)[[:space:]]
the[[:space:]]+(section|subsection|document|doc|guide|runbook|register|inventory|spec|readme|chapter|appendix|page|file|note|list|report|matrix)[[:space:]]+(above|below)[[:space:]]+(is|are|was|were|stays|remains)[[:space:]]
# How the artifact was authored, which is the same claim wearing different grammar
(above|below)[[:space:]]+(is|are)[[:space:]]+(recorded|generated|maintained|authored|curated|written)[[:space:]]+by[[:space:]]+hand
(stays|remains|is|are)[[:space:]]+hand-(maintained|written|authored|curated|recorded)
